    "body": "Aerial view of the Simandou project area. (Image courtesy of Rio Tinto).\nBy: Staff Writer November 27, 2026\nRio Tinto (NYSE, LSE, ASX: RIO) has shipped the first Europe-bound iron ore cargo from Guinea’s giant Simandou project , expanding its market beyond China.\nThe Tower Point left Morebaya anchorage on Nov. 23 carrying about 79,100 tonnes of Simandou iron ore fines sold by SimFer, commodities data and analytics firm Kpler said Friday . SimFer is the Rio Tinto-led venture that operates Simandou’s two southern mining blocks.\nThe vessel is expected in Dunkirk, France, on Dec. 4. Luxembourg-based ArcelorMittal (NYSE: MT) operates a steelworks there. A second Europe-bound cargo is being loaded aboard the True Caspian for delivery to Gijón, Spain, where ArcelorMittal also has steelmaking operations, according to Kpler.\nSimandou began exporting ore in December 2025, with its initial cargoes going to China. The high-grade deposit offers steelmakers an alternative source of ore as they seek to reduce emissions.\nSource: Kpler Insight.\nArcelorMittal, the world’s second-largest steelmaker after China Baowu Steel Group, produces nearly 50 million tonnes of iron ore annually from its own mines, covering more than 70% of its requirements.\nRamp-up\nSimFer reached an annual production rate of about 10 million tonnes in the third quarter, Rio Tinto said. The venture targets a rate of 60 million tonnes a year in the second half of 2028; the wider Simandou development is designed to export up to 120 million tonnes annually.\nGuinea’s monthly iron ore exports surpassed 5 million tonnes for the first time in October. Year-to-date shipments had exceeded 22 million tonnes, shipping association BIMCO reported in a Nov. 18 update.\nRio Tinto and a Chinalco-led consortium are developing Simandou’s southern blocks through SimFer, while Winning Consortium Simandou is developing the northern blocks. Guinea holds 15% of each mining venture. The partners share a rail and port infrastructure system.\nWealth fund\nThe growing trade comes as Guinea prepares to set aside mining revenue for long-term investment. The country’s National Assembly approved legislation establishing a sovereign wealth fund on Nov. 25. The text will go to President Mamadi Doumbouya for promulgation, the government said Thursday.\nThe fund’s initial capital target is $1 billion (C$1.4 billion), to be raised over no more than five years, according to the presidency. Planned funding includes 30% of taxes and other state revenue from Simandou and 5% of revenue from new extractive projects, the government said.\nUnder Guinea’s Simandou 2040 development programme, the fund would invest in infrastructure, energy, industry and local mineral processing, while building savings for future generations. A separate reserve would help cushion public finances against commodity price swings, according to official accounts.\nThe state-owned fund would have nine directors, including seven independent members. Its proposed safeguards include annual external audits and published financial statements. Withdrawals from savings for future generations would require approval from both the board and parliament and could not pay for routine government spending, the government said.\nPrint\nTAG: iron ore steel\nCOMMODITY: Iron Ore\nREGION: Africa Europe\nCOMPANY: ArcelorMittal Chinalco Rio Tinto\nRepublish this article",
